		bodyHtml: `<p>Paul has just thanked the church for sending help: &ldquo;I rejoiced greatly in the Lord that at last you renewed your concern for me.&rdquo; (Philippians 4:10). Then he heads off a misunderstanding: &ldquo;Not that I speak from need&mdash;I&rsquo;ve learned to be content in any circumstance.&rdquo; (Philippians 4:11). He goes on: &ldquo;I know how to be brought low, and I know how to overflow. In any and every circumstance I&rsquo;ve learned the secret: full and hungry, overflowing and lacking.&rdquo; (Philippians 4:12).</p><p>The whole passage is about circumstances: full or hungry, overflowing or lacking. Paul says contentment is something he <em>learned</em>, a secret, and not a mood that arrives on its own. Verse 13 is the next sentence in that same thought.</p>`,
